# Line endings, and what is binary. E14-08 owns this file.
#
# ── Why the line below is load-bearing ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform. `core.autocrlf`
# is a per-machine setting, and this project cannot depend on four developers
# configuring it identically - .gitattributes is the only mechanism that makes a
# Windows clone and a Linux clone produce the same diffs.
#
# It is also a build gate, not a tidiness preference. `.editorconfig` sets
# `end_of_line = lf`, and `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` (E14-02, and a CI step
# in E14-07) fails with ENDOFLINE errors against a CRLF working tree. Measured on
# 2026-08-24 with `core.autocrlf=true` and no .gitattributes: converting one .cs file
# to CRLF failed the format check with exit 2. Left alone, that means CI on Linux
# passes while a fresh Windows clone fails - the worst shape a build failure can take,
# because the machine that reports green is not the machine anyone is working on.
#
# There is no tool-churn cost. Every generator in the chain already writes LF: Godot
# writes project.godot, .tscn, .uid and the Dlo.Game.csproj it generates as LF, and
# `dotnet new sln` / `dotnet sln add` write LF. No CRLF exception is needed anywhere,
# which is why there is not one below.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Godot and content: text, and it has to stay text ────────────────────────
#
# `.tres` is deliberately NOT in LFS (arch §7). Content files must stay diffable or
# E13's authoring pipeline loses code review entirely - a routing policy you cannot
# read in a PR is a routing policy nobody checks. A careless LFS wildcard is exactly
# how that breaks, so the content formats are marked explicitly here rather than left
# to Git's binary detection.
#
# `.import` sidecars stay tracked and diffable too: they carry the stable `uid://`
# that `.tres` content files resolve against (see .gitignore).

*.tres          text
*.tscn          text
*.godot         text
*.import        text
*.uid           text
*.cfg           text
*.gd            text
*.gltf          text

# ── Source, build and docs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

*.cs            text diff=csharp
*.csproj        text
*.props         text
*.targets       text
*.sln           text
*.json          text
*.md            text
*.yml           text
*.yaml          text
.editorconfig   text
.gitattributes  text
.gitignore      text

# ── Git LFS: binary art and audio ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Wider than what the repo contains today, on purpose. A binary committed outside LFS
# cannot be un-committed without rewriting history, so the cost of listing a format
# early is one line and the cost of listing it late is a history rewrite. This is one
# of the few places where guessing ahead is cheaper than waiting for the need.
#
# Most placeholder .png and .wav never reach LFS at all: E17-03 generates them before
# the build and .gitignore excludes them, so a fresh clone works with no `git lfs pull`
# whatsoever. LFS is here for commissioned art - authored, not derived, and therefore
# not regenerable.

*.png           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.jpg           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.jpeg          filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.exr           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.wav           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.ogg           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.mp3           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.glb           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.fbx           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.blend         filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.ttf           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.otf           filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
